    "https://www.gwu.jobs/postings/129488": {
      "title": "Assistant Program Director, Marketing360",
      "description": "George Washington Employment Site | Assistant Program Director, Marketing360 Skip to Main Content SEARCH JOBS Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Search Job Libraries Log In /Create Account Help Assistant Program Director, Marketing360 Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Employer will not sponsor for employment Visa status Posting Details I. JOB OVERVIEW Job Description Summary: The F. David Fowler Career Center ( FDFCC ) supports GW Business students and alumni in developing the skills, knowledge, and connections needed to navigate their careers. Through career programming, guidance, employer and alumni engagement, and experiential learning, the Center connects students with opportunities to explore career paths, build skills, and gain experience. Marketing360 is a career development program for undergraduate and graduate students interested in marketing. The program helps students understand the range of career paths available in marketing, build technical and professional skills, and develop relationships with alumni and industry professionals. Programming includes workshops, networking, industry engagement, and experiential learning. The Assistant Program Director, Marketing360 leads the day-to-day management and continued development of the program. The role combines program operations, student-focused communications and engagement, external relationship building, experiential programming, and career guidance. Marketing360 culminates in cross-functional, semester-long pro bono projects that allow students to apply what they have learned to real-world client challenges in a team-based environment. Key Responsibilities Include: Program Management and Development Manage the day-to-day operations and ongoing development of Marketing360, ensuring programming, communications, events, and student experiences are organized, timely, and aligned with program goals. Design and deliver a two-semester program that helps undergraduate and graduate students explore marketing career paths, build technical and soft skills, and develop professional networks. Plan and execute workshops, presentations, networking events, site visits, and other student-facing career development activities. Develop clear, engaging, student-focused messaging and communications to promote programming, opportunities, expectations, and resources. Manage program operations, including budgets, contracts, scheduling, logistics, records, student participation, communications, and other administrative processes. Coordinate the Marketing360 pro bono project experience, including project planning, student teams, client communication, timelines, and logistics. Oversee student workers supporting the program and manage program resources, presentations, guides, templates, and other student-facing materials. Track participation and career-related outcomes and use data and feedback to evaluate the program and improve delivery. Identify and implement process improvements that strengthen program effectiveness and the student experience. Collaboration and Relationship Building Build and maintain relationships with industry professionals, employers, alumni, and other external partners to create a strong pipeline of speakers, mentors, networking opportunities, and pro bono project partners. Identify and cultivate new relationships that expand students’ exposure to marketing careers, industries, organizations, and professional opportunities. Maintain strong relationships with Marketing faculty and identify opportunities to connect academic learning with career exploration and professional development. Partner with colleagues across the Fowler Career Center and GW School of Business to strengthen student programming and identify opportunities for collaboration. Student Career Advising Provide individualized career advising to undergraduate and graduate students across industries. Help students assess their interests, skills, and goals and identify appropriate marketing career paths and opportunities. Mentor students on resumes, interviewing, professional branding, networking, and other career-readiness skills. Connect students with relevant career resources, alumni, employers, and other opportunities. Performs other related duties as assigned. The omission of specific duties does not preclude the supervisor from assigning duties that are logically related to the position. This is a hybrid position with a combination of on-campus and remote work. The role requires regular on-campus presence for student programming, meetings, events, and other responsibilities. Minimum Qualifications: Qualified candidates will hold a Bachelor’s degree in an appropriate area of specialization plus 2 years of relevant professional experience, or, a Master’s degree or higher in a relevant area of study. Degree must be conferred by the start date of the position. Degree requirements may be substituted with an equivalent combination of education, training and experience. Additional Required Licenses/Certifications/Posting Specific Minimum Qualifications: Preferred Qualifications: Experience in marketing, marketing communications, career development, higher education, program management, or a related field is desired. Demonstrated experience managing programs, projects, events, or other complex initiatives with multiple stakeholders and deadlines. Strong organizational skills and exceptional attention to detail. Strong fluency with office and productivity technology, including Google Workspace and Microsoft Word, Excel, and PowerPoint and/or Canva. Ability to think strategically while operating effectively as an independent contributor and managing day-to-day execution. Demonstrated ability to build and maintain productive relationships with external professionals, employers, alumni, faculty, and other stakeholders. Experience developing and delivering workshops, events, or other student-facing programming. Strong written and verbal communication skills, including the ability to develop clear and engaging student-focused communications. Experience with career coaching, student advising, talent development, or related areas preferred. Experience with data collection, program assessment, and reporting preferred. Hiring Range $58,992.50 - $97,373.72 GW Staff Approach to Pay How is pay for new employees determined at GW? Healthcare Benefits GW offers a comprehensive benefit package that includes medical, dental, vision, life & disability insurance, time off & leave, retirement savings, tuition, well-being and various voluntary benefits. For program details and eligibility, please visit https://hr.gwu.edu/benefits-programs. II. JOB DETAILS Campus Location: Foggy Bottom, Washington, D.C. College/School/Department: School of Business (SB) Family Student Affairs Sub-Family Career Services Stream Individual Contributor Level Level 2 Full-Time/Part-Time: Full-Time Hours Per Week: 40 Work Schedule: Monday - Friday, 9:00 A.M. - 6:00 P.M. Will this job require the employee to work on site? Yes Employee Onsite Status Hybrid Telework: Yes Required Background Check: Criminal History Screening, Education/Degree/Certifications Verification, Social Security Number Trace, and Sex Offender Registry Search Special Instructions to Applicants: Employer will not sponsor for employment Visa status Internal Applicants Only? No Posting Number: S014532 Job Open Date: Job Close Date: If temporary, grant funded, Sponsored Project funded or limited term appointment, position funded until: Background Screening Successful Completion of a Background Screening will be required as a condition of hire. EEO Statement: The university is an Equal Employment Opportunity employer that does not unlawfully discriminate in any of its programs or activities on the basis of race, color, religion, sex, national origin, age, disability, veteran status, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, or on any other basis prohibited by applicable law. Posting Specific Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Are you currently employed with The George Washington University? No Yes * For current GW employees, have you completed your Introductory Employment Period (IEP)? (As a reminder, employees in their IEP are not eligible to apply for other internal university staff and research positions until the IEP is complete.) Yes, IEP complete No, still in IEP N/a - not a current GW employee * What is your salary range expectation? (Open Ended Question) * Which areas of marketing do you have direct professional experience in (e.g., brand strategy, digital marketing, marketing analytics, or content creation)? Please indicate the number of years of experience you have in each area. (Open Ended Question) * Rate and describe your proficiency level with Microsoft Suite including Docs, Sheets, PowerPoint, Chat and Calendars (e.g., basic data entry vs. advanced formulas, pivot tables, and reporting). (Open Ended Question) * Briefly describe a situation where you demonstrated initiative, resourcefulness, and effective communication while working with a team. What was your contribution, and what was the outcome? (Open Ended Question) Documents needed to Apply Required Documents Resume Cover Letter Optional Documents Human Resource Management & Development 2013 H Street, NW, 3rd Floor Washington, DC 20006 Phone: 202-994-8500 Fax: 202-994-9680 Email: askhrmd@gwu.edu Recognition_Toolkit Talent@GWLogin Privacy Notice Connect with us To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
